    "original_title": "Xusuusta Qaraxii Hargaha iyo Saamaha",
    "slug": "somali-diaspora-marks-15-years-since-deadly-2011-mogadishu-bombing",
    "summary": "The 2011 attack on the former Ministry of Livestock headquarters in Mogadishu killed nearly 100 people, mostly students. Fifteen years on, the Somali diaspora continues to honor the victims and reflect on the lasting impact.",
    "content": "October 4, 2026, marks 15 years since a devastating bombing at the former headquarters of the Ministry of Livestock and Animal Husbandry in Mogadishu. The attack, carried out on October 4, 2011, claimed the lives of nearly 100 people, the majority of whom were students, parents, and civilians. It remains one of the deadliest single incidents in Somalia's recent history.\n\nThe bombing occurred during a period of intense conflict and instability in Somalia, when armed groups frequently targeted government installations and public spaces. The Ministry of Livestock, located in a busy area of the capital, was among several institutions hit during that era. The victims included young people pursuing education, a loss that resonated deeply across Somali communities at home and abroad.\n\nIn the years since, the site has been remembered through annual commemorations, though no permanent national memorial has been established. Survivors and families of the victims have called for greater recognition of their suffering, while authorities have occasionally pledged to honor the fallen. The absence of a formal monument leaves many feeling that the tragedy has not been fully acknowledged in official narratives.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the 2011 bombing represents a painful chapter that many carry personally. Diaspora members often lost relatives or friends in the attack, and the event has become a symbol of the broader toll of decades of violence. Remittances and community support from abroad have helped some families cope, but the emotional scars persist. Annual gatherings in cities like Minneapolis, London, and Nairobi serve as spaces for collective mourning and reflection.\n\nAs Somalia continues to rebuild, the legacy of the 2011 attack underscores the importance of remembrance and accountability. The diaspora's role in preserving the memory of victims is seen as vital, ensuring that those who died are not forgotten. While the country has made progress in security and governance, the pain of that day remains a reminder of the work still needed to achieve lasting peace.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/04/xusuusta-qaraxii-hargaha-iyo-saamaha/",
